const TOPICS = [];
const TP = (o) => TOPICS.push(o);

TP({id:'four-views', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Four Views', ch:[1,4,6,11,13,17,20,22],
 lead:`Christians have read Revelation in four main ways. Each takes the Bible seriously, and each has been held by careful, faithful people. Most readers today mix two or more. Every chapter page on this site shows how each view reads that chapter.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The four views side by side', table:[['','Preterist','Historicist','Futurist','Idealist'],['Main idea','Fulfilled mostly in the first centuries','A map of church history','Mostly still future','Timeless patterns'],['Chapters 4–19','The fall of Jerusalem (AD 70) and/or Rome','From the apostles to the end, in order','A future seven-year tribulation','God’s war with evil in every age'],['The beast','Nero or Rome','The papacy (Protestant), or another power','A future world ruler','Any power that demands worship'],['Babylon','Jerusalem or Rome','Papal Rome','A future city or system','Human society against God'],['The thousand years','Usually the church age','A period of church history','A future reign on earth','The church age'],['Early roots','Eusebius; Alcázar (1614)','Joachim of Fiore (1100s)','Irenaeus, Hippolytus; Ribera (1590)','Tyconius, Augustine (300s–400s)'],['Well-known holders','R. C. Sproul, Kenneth Gentry, many Catholic scholars','Luther, Isaac Newton, Jonathan Edwards, early Adventists','John Walvoord, Hal Lindsey, Tim LaHaye','William Milligan, many modern scholars']]},
  ['Preterist ("already happened")',`From Latin <i>praeter</i>, "past." Most of the book was fulfilled in the first centuries: the fall of Jerusalem in AD 70 and the conflict with pagan Rome. <b>Partial</b> preterists still expect a future return of Christ and resurrection (chapters 19–22). <b>Full</b> preterists believe even those were fulfilled, a view almost all churches reject. Early forms appear in Eusebius; the Jesuit scholar Luis de Alcázar (1614) gave it its modern shape. Its strength: it takes seriously that the book was written to real churches about things that would "soon take place" (1:1).`],
  ['Historicist ("unfolding through history")',`The book is a coded map of church history from John's day to the end, often using a "day for a year" rule, so 1,260 days become 1,260 years. Joachim of Fiore (1100s) began it; it became the standard Protestant view from the Reformation to the 1800s: Luther, Isaac Newton, Jonathan Edwards, Matthew Henry, and the founders of the Seventh-day Adventist church. Its weakness: its interpreters have never agreed on the map, and each tends to place the end in his own day.`],
  ['Futurist ("mostly still future")',`Chapters 4–22 describe events still to come: a tribulation, the Antichrist, Christ's return, and a 1,000-year reign. Early church writers such as Irenaeus and Hippolytus held a simple futurism. The Jesuit Francisco Ribera (1590) revived it. In the 1830s John Nelson Darby joined it to dispensationalism and a pre-tribulation rapture, and the <i>Scofield Reference Bible</i> (1909), Hal Lindsey's <i>The Late Great Planet Earth</i> (1970) and the <i>Left Behind</i> novels (1995–2007) made it the most popular American view.`],
  ['Idealist ("a timeless pattern")',`The book is a symbolic picture of the battle between God and evil in every age, not a timeline. Its roots go back to Tyconius and Augustine (late 300s). Many modern scholars hold an "eclectic" view: the book spoke first to Rome, describes patterns that recur in every age, and points to a real future end. G. K. Beale and Craig Keener are examples.`],
  ['The Catholic and Protestant fight',`The preterist and futurist views were both shaped in the Counter-Reformation. Protestants said the pope was the beast and Rome was Babylon. Catholic scholars answered: Ribera put the beast in the far future, and Alcázar put it in the distant past. Either way, it was not the pope.`]],
 common:[`"Literal" readers are futurists, and everyone else "spiritualizes."`,`You have to pick one view.`],
 uncommon:[`Every view reads some things literally and some symbolically; the question is which.`,`Many scholars combine all four: first-century meaning (preterist), recurring pattern (idealist), and final fulfillment (futurist).`],
 claude:{title:'Ask which view the book itself models', body:`When Revelation explains its own symbols (1:20; 17:9–18), it always points to something in John's own world (churches, a city, kings) and never to a date. That favors reading it first through first-century eyes. But the book also insists on a real ending: a real return, real resurrection, a real new creation (19–22). My reading is that the book trains you to see your own Rome while waiting for the real end, which combines the preterist, idealist and futurist instincts in that order.`, refs:'1:20; 17:9–18; 1:3; 22:10; Daniel 12:4'},
 links:[['Preterism','Preterism'],['Historicism','Historicism (Christianity)'],['Futurism','Futurism (Christianity)'],['Idealism','Idealism (Christian eschatology)']],
 sources:['gregg','keener','beale']});

TP({id:'apocalyptic', kind:'Ideas', title:'What Is an "Apocalypse"?', ch:[1],
 lead:`<i>Apokalypsis</i> means "unveiling," and it is the first word of the book (1:1). It was also the name later given to a kind of Jewish writing popular from about 200 BC to AD 100. Revelation is the most famous example, and knowing the family helps explain its style.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Revelation’s family', table:[['Book','Date (approx.)','Features'],['Daniel 7–12','500s or 160s BC','Four beasts, a son of man, numbered times, angel interpreters'],['1 Enoch','300 BC – AD 1','Heavenly journeys, fallen angels, a Son of Man, an "Animal Apocalypse" of history'],['Jubilees','160s BC','History in numbered periods, heavenly tablets'],['4 Ezra (2 Esdras 3–14)','About AD 100','An eagle for Rome, a lion for the Messiah, laments over Jerusalem'],['2 Baruch','About AD 100','Jerusalem’s fall, hidden temple vessels, the Messiah’s reign'],['Apocalypse of Abraham','About AD 100','A heavenly throne vision, a fallen angel'],['Shepherd of Hermas','100s AD','A Christian apocalypse of visions and parables']]},
  ['Common features',`Visions and dreams; heavenly journeys; angel guides who explain what is seen; symbolic animals for empires; numbered periods of time; cosmic battles between good and evil; and a final judgment and new world. Most were written for people under pressure from great empires: Babylon, Persia, the Seleucid kings or Rome.`],
  ['How Revelation is different',`Most apocalypses claim to be written by an ancient hero such as Enoch, Ezra or Baruch. John uses his own name and writes to real churches he knows. Revelation is also a letter (1:4) and a prophecy (1:3), and it tells readers not to seal it up (22:10), unlike Daniel (Daniel 12:4). And its center is not a coming hero but a Lamb who has already been slaughtered and raised.`],
  ['Why the symbols?',`Symbols let writers say dangerous things about rulers without naming them, and they help readers see the spiritual reality behind events, the way a political cartoon shows what a photograph cannot. A modern reader who sees an eagle or a bear in a cartoon knows it means a nation; ancient readers knew that beasts meant empires.`]],
 common:[`"Apocalypse" means the end of the world.`],
 uncommon:[`"Apocalypse" means an unveiling. The point is seeing clearly, not destruction.`],
 links:[['Apocalyptic literature','Apocalyptic literature'],['Book of Enoch','Book of Enoch'],['2 Esdras','2 Esdras'],['2 Baruch','2 Baruch']],
 sources:['bauckham','koester','aune']});

TP({id:'structure', kind:'Ideas', title:'How the Book Is Built', ch:[6,8,9,11,15,16],
 lead:`The middle of Revelation is built from three sets of seven: seals (6:1–8:1), trumpets (8:2–11:19) and bowls (15:1–16:21). How they relate, and how they fit the rest of the book, shapes how you read the whole thing.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'An outline of the book', table:[['Chapters','Section','What happens'],['1','Prologue and first vision','The risen Jesus among the lampstands'],['2–3','Seven letters','Messages to the seven churches'],['4–5','The throne room','God on the throne; the Lamb takes the scroll'],['6:1–8:1','Seven seals','Horsemen, martyrs, earthquake; interlude: the 144,000 and the great crowd (7)'],['8:2–11:19','Seven trumpets','Plagues on a third; interlude: the little scroll and two witnesses (10:1–11:14)'],['12–14','The great conflict','The woman and dragon, two beasts, the Lamb on Zion, the harvests'],['15–16','Seven bowls','The final plagues; Armageddon'],['17:1–19:10','Babylon','The prostitute, her fall, the lament, the hallelujahs'],['19:11–20:15','The final victory','The rider, the thousand years, the last judgment'],['21:1–22:5','New creation','The bride, the city, the river and tree'],['22:6–21','Epilogue','"I am coming soon"']]},
  ['One after another',`Twenty-one events in a row. Common in popular futurism. Its weakness: the sixth seal already sounds like the end of the world (6:12–17), and so does the seventh trumpet (11:15–18).`],
  ['Nested (telescoping)',`The seventh seal opens into the seven trumpets, and the seventh trumpet into the seven bowls. Many futurists hold this.`],
  ['Retold (recapitulation)',`Each set covers the same period from a new angle, ending each time at the end, with growing intensity: a fourth (6:8), a third (8:7–12), then everything (16:3). Victorinus of Pettau, who wrote the oldest surviving commentary on Revelation around AD 260, taught this. Support: each seventh ends with thunder, lightning and an earthquake (8:5; 11:19; 16:18), getting bigger each time.`],
  ['A tale of two cities',`Another way to see the shape: the book moves from seven real cities (2–3) to two symbolic ones, Babylon (17–18) and the new Jerusalem (21–22), introduced with the same words (17:1; 21:9). The reader is a citizen of one and is asked to choose.`]],
 common:[`Revelation is a strict timeline, chapter by chapter.`],
 uncommon:[`Revelation may be more like a set of camera angles on the same events, each closer than the last.`],
 claude:{title:'The three sevens follow the three layers of the tabernacle',body:`The seals begin at the altar outside (6:9), the trumpets begin at the gold incense altar inside the Holy Place (8:3; 9:13), and the bowls come straight out of the sanctuary itself, "the tent of the testimony," after the ark has been seen (11:19; 15:5–8). Each set moves one step deeper into God's house. That fits a recapitulation reading: the same history, judged from ever closer to God's presence. Scholars note the temple settings; reading them as a deliberate inward progression is my own proposal.`, refs:'6:9; 8:3; 9:13; 11:19; 15:5–8; Exodus 40:1–8'},
 links:[['Seven seals','Seven seals'],['Seven trumpets','Seven trumpets'],['Seven bowls','Seven bowls'],['Victorinus of Pettau','Victorinus of Pettau']],
 sources:['bauckham','beale','gregg']});

TP({id:'tribulation', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Great Tribulation', ch:[1,2,3,7,11,13],
 lead:`<i>Thlipsis</i> means pressure, suffering, distress. Revelation uses it five times, starting with John's own life: "your brother, who shares with you in the suffering" (1:9).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'"Tribulation" in Revelation', table:[['Verse','Who','Context'],['1:9','John','Already sharing it with his readers'],['2:9','Smyrna','"I know your suffering and your poverty"'],['2:10','Smyrna','"You will suffer for ten days"'],['2:22','Jezebel’s followers','"Great suffering" unless they repent'],['7:14','The great crowd','"They have come out of the great tribulation"']],
   note:'Every use outside 7:14 is about what churches were already facing or about to face.'},
  ['The seven-year idea',`The popular "seven-year tribulation" comes from Daniel's seventieth "week" (Daniel 9:24–27), joined to Revelation's 3½-year periods (11:2–3; 12:6; 13:5) and Jesus's words about "great tribulation, such as has not been from the beginning of the world" (Matthew 24:21). Revelation itself never says "seven years." Futurists see a gap of the whole church age between Daniel's 69th and 70th weeks; others see the 70th week fulfilled in Christ's ministry and the following years.`],
  ['Jesus’s words',`In Matthew 24, Mark 13 and Luke 21, Jesus speaks both of Jerusalem's destruction ("when you see Jerusalem surrounded by armies," Luke 21:20) and of his coming. Preterists read the "great tribulation" as AD 66–70; futurists as a future period; many read both, the first as a preview of the second.`],
  {h:'When are believers "caught up"?', table:[['View','When','Key texts'],['Pre-tribulation','Before the tribulation begins','3:10; 4:1; 1 Thessalonians 1:10; 5:9'],['Mid-tribulation','At the halfway point','11:12; the "last trumpet" (1 Corinthians 15:52)'],['Pre-wrath','Before the final outpouring of wrath','6:17; Matthew 24:29–31'],['Post-tribulation','At Christ’s return after it','Matthew 24:29–31; 1 Thessalonians 4:17; Revelation 20:4']]}],
 common:[`Christians will escape all end-time suffering.`],
 uncommon:[`In Revelation the faithful are protected from God's wrath but not from the world's hostility. The martyrs "came out of" the great tribulation by going through it (7:14).`],
 claude:{title:'"Kept from the hour" may mean kept through it',body:`The promise to Philadelphia uses <i>tērēsō ek</i>, "I will keep you out of" the hour of testing (3:10). John uses the same verb and preposition in only one other place in his writings: Jesus's prayer, "I am not asking you to take them out of the world, but to keep them from the evil one" (John 17:15). There, "keep from" explicitly does not mean "remove." This has been noted by post-tribulation writers such as Robert Gundry; I find it the strongest single word study on the question.`, refs:'3:10; John 17:15; 7:14'},
 views:{P:`The suffering of AD 64–70: Nero's persecution and the Jewish war.`,H:`Long periods of persecution in church history, especially under the papacy.`,F:`A future seven-year period of unequaled trouble, mostly after the church is taken.`,I:`The ongoing suffering of the church in a hostile world, intensifying at the end.`},
 links:[['Great Tribulation','Great Tribulation'],['Prophecy of Seventy Weeks','Prophecy of Seventy Weeks'],['Rapture','Rapture']],
 sources:['gregg','ladd','walvoord']});

TP({id:'rapture', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Rapture', ch:[3,4,11,12,14],
 lead:`The belief that believers will be "caught up" to meet Christ. The word comes from the Latin translation of 1 Thessalonians 4:17, <i>rapiemur</i>. The idea of being caught up is biblical; the debate is about when it happens and how it relates to Christ's return.`,
 sections:[
  ['Where it comes from',`1 Thessalonians 4:16–17 (caught up to meet the Lord in the air), 1 Corinthians 15:51–52 (changed at the last trumpet), John 14:2–3 ("I will come back and take you to be with me"), and Matthew 24:40–41 ("one will be taken and the other left").`],
  ['History',`Most of church history expected one return of Christ, with believers rising to meet him as he comes. The idea of a separate, earlier, secret rapture before a tribulation was developed by John Nelson Darby in the 1830s, among the Plymouth Brethren in Britain. It spread through the Niagara Bible Conferences, the <i>Scofield Reference Bible</i> (1909), Dallas Theological Seminary, Hal Lindsey's books and the <i>Left Behind</i> series. Some find earlier hints, such as a sermon attributed to Pseudo-Ephraem (perhaps 500s) and the Baptist Morgan Edwards (1788).`],
  ['In Revelation',`Revelation never uses the word for the church. Its only "caught up" (<i>harpazō</i>) is the male child, Jesus, snatched up to God's throne (12:5). Readers see the rapture pictured at 4:1 (pre-tribulation), 11:12 (mid-tribulation), 14:14–16 (the grain harvest), or at Christ's return in 19:11–14. The word "church" disappears after chapter 3, which pre-tribulation writers take as a sign the church is gone; others note that "God's people," "saints" and "servants" appear throughout.`],
  ['"Meet" the Lord',`"Meet" in 1 Thessalonians 4:17 (<i>apantēsis</i>) was the word for a city's leaders going out to welcome a visiting king or general and escorting him back into the city. Many scholars think that means believers rise to welcome Jesus as he comes to earth, not to leave with him.`]],
 common:[`The rapture is clearly described in Revelation 4:1.`,`The pre-tribulation rapture is the historic Christian view.`],
 uncommon:[`The rapture and the second coming are one event, with believers rising to welcome the returning king.`],
 claude:{title:'Revelation’s model of "going up" is the Lamb’s own path',body:`The only person caught up in Revelation is the Son (12:5), and the only people who go up to heaven are the two witnesses, after faithful witness, death and resurrection (11:7–12). The 144,000 "follow the Lamb wherever he goes" (14:4). My reading: Revelation frames the hope as following Jesus's own path (witness, suffering, vindication, ascension) rather than escaping before trouble. That is closest to post-tribulation and idealist readings.`, refs:'12:5; 11:7–12; 14:4; 1 Thessalonians 4:17'},
 links:[['Rapture','Rapture'],['John Nelson Darby','John Nelson Darby'],['Pre-tribulation rapture','Pre-tribulation rapture'],['Post-tribulation rapture','Post-tribulation rapture']],
 sources:['weber','ladd','walvoord','gregg']});

TP({id:'millennium', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Thousand Years (the Millennium)', ch:[20],
 lead:`<i>Chilia etē</i>, "a thousand years," appears six times, all in 20:2–7. The Latin <i>mille annum</i> gives us "millennium." Few passages have shaped Christian hope and politics more.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The three views', table:[['','Premillennial','Amillennial','Postmillennial'],['Christ returns','Before the thousand years','After the thousand years (one return, then the end)','After the thousand years'],['The thousand years','A future reign on earth','The present church age','A future golden age of the gospel'],['Satan "bound"','Literally, in the future','By Christ’s victory at the cross (Matthew 12:29)','Increasingly, as the gospel spreads'],['First resurrection','Bodily resurrection of believers','New birth, or the soul’s life with Christ after death','Spiritual renewal of the church'],['Early holders','Papias, Justin, Irenaeus','Tyconius, Augustine','Some medieval writers'],['Later holders','Dispensationalists; George Ladd','Catholic, Orthodox, Lutheran, much Reformed','Jonathan Edwards, Charles Hodge, many 1800s Protestants']]},
  ['Premillennial',`Christ returns before a literal 1,000-year reign on earth. This was the view of many early writers: Papias, Justin Martyr and Irenaeus, who imagined a world of astonishing abundance. <b>Historic premillennialism</b> (George Ladd) sees one people of God; <b>dispensational premillennialism</b> sees the millennium fulfilling promises to national Israel, with a rebuilt temple.`],
  ['Amillennial',`The thousand years is the present age between Christ's two comings. Satan was bound by Christ's victory (Matthew 12:29; Luke 10:18), and the saints reign with Christ now in heaven. Augustine made this the standard view (<i>City of God</i> 20.7–9), after first holding a premillennial view himself; it is held by Catholic, Orthodox, Lutheran and many Reformed churches.`],
  ['Postmillennial',`Christ returns after a long golden age brought about by the spread of the gospel. Popular among Puritans and 1800s American Protestants (Jonathan Edwards, Charles Hodge), it fueled missions and social reform. It faded after the world wars, though some still hold it.`],
  ['The millennium in history',`Expectations of a thousand-year kingdom have inspired movements both peaceful and violent. In 1534–35 radical Anabaptists seized the city of Münster, declared it the New Jerusalem, and were crushed. In the 1600s the English Fifth Monarchy Men expected Christ's reign soon. Historians use "millenarian" for any movement that expects a coming perfect age.`]],
 common:[`The Bible clearly teaches a future 1,000-year reign.`],
 uncommon:[`The doctrine rests mostly on one chapter of symbolic writing. Everyone, premillennial or not, reads some of chapter 20 symbolically (a chain that binds a spirit, a key, an abyss).`],
 claude:{title:'Isaiah 24 may be the blueprint for Revelation 20',body:`Isaiah 24:21–23 describes the powers of heaven and the kings of earth gathered "like prisoners in a pit," shut up "and after many days punished," followed by the Lord reigning "on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem" in glory. That is the sequence of Revelation 20: bound in a pit, a long period, a release and punishment, and God's reign in his city. Premillennial writers cite Isaiah 24:22. My suggestion is that the whole shape of Revelation 20, not just the prison, is modeled on Isaiah 24–25, which also gives us "he will swallow up death forever" and "God will wipe away tears" (Isaiah 25:8), both used in Revelation 21:4. If so, John is reading Isaiah's "many days" as his "thousand years," a long but symbolic period.`, refs:'Isaiah 24:21–23; Isaiah 25:8; 20:1–10; 21:4'},
 links:[['Millennialism','Millennialism'],['Premillennialism','Premillennialism'],['Amillennialism','Amillennialism'],['Postmillennialism','Postmillennialism'],['Münster rebellion','Münster rebellion']],
 sources:['gregg','augustine','ladd','beale']});

TP({id:'second-coming', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Second Coming', ch:[1,3,16,19,22],
 lead:`The return of Jesus is the book's frame. It opens with "Look! He is coming with the clouds" (1:7) and closes with "Yes, I am coming soon" (22:20). In between, "coming" is used in several ways.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'"I am coming" in Revelation', table:[['Verse','To whom','Kind of coming'],['1:7','Everyone','With the clouds; every eye will see him'],['2:5','Ephesus','To remove their lampstand, unless they repent'],['2:16','Pergamum','To fight the false teachers'],['2:25','Thyatira','"Hold on until I come"'],['3:3','Sardis','Like a thief, if they do not wake up'],['3:11','Philadelphia','"I am coming soon. Hold on"'],['16:15','Everyone','Like a thief; blessed is the one who stays awake'],['19:11–16','The world','The rider on the white horse'],['22:7, 12, 20','The readers','"I am coming soon"']]},
  ['Debates',`Preterists distinguish comings in judgment (like AD 70) from the final coming. Futurists see 19:11 as one visible event after the tribulation, sometimes separated from an earlier rapture. Idealists see Christ "coming" in judgment and mercy throughout history, with a final coming at the end. Almost every Christian tradition confesses, in the words of the Nicene Creed, "He will come again in glory to judge the living and the dead."`],
  {h:'Dates that came and went', table:[['Year','Who','What was expected'],['About 170','Montanus and his prophets','The New Jerusalem descending at Pepuza in Phrygia'],['1000','Popular fear (later much exaggerated)','The end of the thousand years'],['1260','Followers of Joachim of Fiore','A new age of the Spirit'],['1534','Anabaptists at Münster','Christ’s kingdom in their city'],['1844','William Miller and the Millerites','Christ’s return on October 22: "the Great Disappointment"'],['1914, 1975','Jehovah’s Witness leaders','The end of the present system'],['1988','Edgar Whisenant, <i>88 Reasons</i>','The rapture during Rosh Hashanah'],['2011','Harold Camping','The rapture on May 21']],
   note:'Jesus said, "About that day or hour no one knows, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father" (Mark 13:32).'}],
 common:[`Every "I am coming" in Revelation means the Second Coming.`],
 uncommon:[`Some are clearly visits of judgment to one church (2:5, 16), which shows "coming" language can be local.`],
 links:[['Second Coming','Second Coming'],['Parousia','Parousia'],['Great Disappointment','Great Disappointment'],['Predictions and claims for the Second Coming','Predictions and claims for the Second Coming']],
 sources:['bauckham','koester','weber']});

TP({id:'day-of-the-lord', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Day of the Lord', ch:[1,6,9,16],
 lead:`In the prophets, "the day of the LORD" is the day God steps in to judge and to rescue. Revelation borrows its imagery heavily, but never uses the exact phrase. The sixth seal (6:12–17) is built almost entirely from day-of-the-Lord passages.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The sixth seal and its sources', table:[['Revelation 6','Prophetic source'],['A great earthquake (6:12)','Joel 2:10; Haggai 2:6'],['Sun black like sackcloth (6:12)','Isaiah 50:3; Joel 2:31'],['Moon like blood (6:12)','Joel 2:31'],['Stars fall like figs from a tree (6:13)','Isaiah 34:4'],['Sky rolled up like a scroll (6:14)','Isaiah 34:4'],['People hide in caves and rocks (6:15)','Isaiah 2:10, 19–21'],['"Fall on us, mountains" (6:16)','Hosea 10:8; Luke 23:30'],['"The great day of their anger has come" (6:17)','Joel 2:11; Zephaniah 1:14–18; Malachi 3:2']]},
  ['In the prophets',`Darkened sun and moon, earthquakes, people hiding in rocks, a day of wrath (Isaiah 2:10–21; Isaiah 13:9–10; Joel 2:1–11, 31; Zephaniah 1:14–18). Many of these "days" came in history: Isaiah 13 uses cosmic language for the fall of Babylon to the Medes, and Isaiah 34 for the judgment of Edom. That is why preterists read Revelation's cosmic signs as the collapse of nations, while futurists expect literal signs in the sky.`],
  ['In Revelation',`"The great day of their anger" (6:17), "the great day of God, the All-Powerful" (16:14). The prophets' day of the Lord was also a day of rescue: "Everyone who calls on the name of the LORD will be saved" (Joel 2:32), which Peter quoted at Pentecost (Acts 2:21).`],
  ['"The Lord’s day" (1:10)',`A different phrase: <i>kyriakē hēmera</i>, "the day belonging to the Lord." Early Christians used it for Sunday, the day of the resurrection (Didache 14:1; Ignatius, <i>To the Magnesians</i> 9:1). A few interpreters, such as E. W. Bullinger, read 1:10 as "I was in the Spirit [transported into] the day of the Lord," the end times; most think John simply says when the vision came.`]],
 views:{P:`The day of the Lord came on Jerusalem in AD 70, as Jesus warned.`,H:`Great judgments in history, such as the fall of Rome or the French Revolution.`,F:`A future period of judgment, including the tribulation and Christ's return.`,I:`The pattern of God's judgment on proud powers in every age, ending in a final day.`},
 links:[['Day of the Lord','Day of the Lord'],['Lord’s Day','Lord’s Day']],
 sources:['beale','bauckham']});

TP({id:'wrath', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Wrath of God', ch:[6,11,14,15,16,19],
 lead:`Revelation speaks openly of God's anger (<i>orgē</i>) and fury (<i>thymos</i>). Its picture is not of a temper but of justice for the victims of the beast and Babylon. Many modern readers find this the hardest part of the book.`,
 sections:[
  ['How it works in the book',`Wrath answers the martyrs' cry, "How long, Sovereign Lord... until you judge?" (6:10). It falls on those who "destroy the earth" (11:18) and who shed the blood of God's people (16:6). The plagues are paired with repeated calls to repent (9:20–21; 16:9, 11). In the songs, God's judgments are called "true and just" (15:3; 16:7; 19:2).`],
  {h:'Justice in the songs', table:[['Verse','Who sings','What they say'],['15:3–4','The victors by the sea','"Just and true are your ways... your righteous acts have been revealed"'],['16:5–6','The angel of the waters','"They poured out the blood of your holy people... you have given them blood to drink, as they deserve"'],['16:7','The altar','"True and just are your judgments"'],['19:1–2','The crowd in heaven','"His judgments are true and just. He has judged the great prostitute who corrupted the earth"']]},
  ['The cup of wine',`"Drink the wine of God's anger, poured full strength into the cup" (14:10) comes from the prophets: "Take from my hand this cup filled with the wine of my wrath and make all the nations drink it" (Jeremiah 25:15; also Isaiah 51:17). Babylon made the nations drink her wine (14:8); now she drinks God's (16:19). Jesus prayed in Gethsemane, "Take this cup from me" (Mark 14:36), and drank it himself.`],
  ['"The wrath of the Lamb"',`A deliberate shock (6:16). Lambs are not angry. The one who judges is the one who was slaughtered for the people he judges, which means his anger cannot be selfish.`],
  ['Why it matters to victims',`Theologians who have lived through war, such as Miroslav Volf from the former Yugoslavia, have argued that belief in God's final judgment is what frees victims from taking revenge themselves. Revelation's martyrs ask God to judge; they never pick up a sword. Christians who follow the Lamb "conquer" by testimony (12:11).`]],
 common:[`God's wrath in Revelation is arbitrary destruction.`],
 uncommon:[`Several bowls give back what was done: those who poured out blood are given blood to drink (16:6). Some scholars describe the plagues as God handing people over to what they chose (compare Romans 1:24–28).`],
 views:{P:`God's judgment on Jerusalem and Rome for persecuting his people.`,H:`God's judgments on corrupt powers through history.`,F:`The future wrath of the tribulation and Christ's return, from which believers are kept.`,I:`God's justice against evil in every age, culminating at the end.`},
 links:[['Wrath of God','Wrath of God'],['Miroslav Volf','Miroslav Volf']],
 sources:['bauckham','gorman','keener']});

TP({id:'church-israel', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Church and Israel', ch:[2,3,7,11,12,14,20,21],
 lead:`Does Revelation see one people of God or two? This question shapes how you read chapters 7, 11, 12, 14, 20 and 21. The <button class="linkish" data-go="israel">Church and Israel</button> study page collects every note on the question.`,
 sections:[
  ['Evidence for two distinct peoples',`The word "church" appears 19 times in chapters 1–3 and then not again until 22:16. The 144,000 are listed tribe by tribe (7:4–8). Jerusalem is central (11:8; 20:9). Paul expects a future turning of Israel: "all Israel will be saved" (Romans 11:25–26).`],
  ['Evidence for one people',`The church is called "a kingdom and priests" (1:6; 5:10), Israel's own title (Exodus 19:6). John hears 144,000 from Israel and then sees a crowd from every nation (7:4, 9). One bride (19:7). One city, with the tribes on the gates and the apostles on the foundations (21:12–14). Paul: "If you belong to Christ, you are Abraham's offspring" (Galatians 3:29).`],
  {h:'The positions', table:[['Position','In brief'],['Dispensational','Two peoples with two sets of promises; Israel’s promises fulfilled in the millennium'],['Progressive dispensational','One people in Christ, but ethnic Israel keeps a distinct future role'],['Historic premillennial','One people, plus a future mass turning of Jews to Jesus'],['Covenant (Reformed)','One people of God across both Testaments; the church continues and fulfills Israel'],['Messianic Jewish','One body in which Jewish believers keep their Jewish identity and calling']]},
  ['A warning from history',`Reading "synagogue of Satan" (2:9; 3:9) or Babylon as "the Jews" has been used to fuel hatred. In context these are local disputes inside the Jewish and Christian community of Asia, written by a Jewish author who loved Israel's Scriptures. After the Holocaust, many churches officially rejected readings that blame or erase the Jewish people.`]],
 common:[`You must choose between "replacement theology" and "two peoples."`],
 uncommon:[`Many positions in between hold that Israel is neither replaced nor kept separate, but enlarged.`],
 claude:{title:'One olive tree, with Israel at the root',body:`Revelation never "replaces" Israel: it names the tribes one by one and writes them on the gates of the final city. But it also never keeps two separate peoples in the final picture: one bride, one city, one Lamb. My reading is closest to Paul's single olive tree (Romans 11:17–24): Israel is the trunk and the nations are grafted in. You enter through Israel's names (21:12) and stand on the apostles' (21:14).`, refs:'21:12–14; Romans 11:17–24; 7:4–9; Ephesians 2:14–16'},
 links:[['Dispensationalism','Dispensationalism'],['Covenant theology','Covenant theology'],['Supersessionism','Supersessionism'],['Messianic Judaism','Messianic Judaism']],
 sources:['gregg','bauckham','walvoord']});

TP({id:'antichrist', kind:'People', title:'The Antichrist', ch:[13,17,19],
 lead:`The word "antichrist" never appears in Revelation. It is found only in John's letters (1 John 2:18, 22; 1 John 4:3; 2 John 7), where John says "many antichrists" have already come. The Greek <i>anti</i> can mean both "against" and "in place of": an antichrist opposes Christ by imitating him.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'How the idea was built', table:[['Source','Figure','Main features'],['Daniel 7:8, 24–25; 8:9–12; 11:36','The "little horn"','Speaks against God, persecutes the saints, changes times and laws'],['Matthew 24:15; Mark 13:14','"The abomination of desolation"','A defiling presence in the holy place'],['2 Thessalonians 2:3–10','"The man of lawlessness"','Sits in God’s temple claiming to be God; false signs'],['1 John 2:18–22; 4:3','"Antichrist," "many antichrists"','Denies that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh'],['Revelation 13; 17','The beast','Healed wound, worshiped, blasphemes, wars on the saints']],
   note:'Irenaeus (about AD 180) and Hippolytus (about 200) were already combining these into one end-time figure.'},
  ['Jewish and early Christian background',`The model for many of these was Antiochus IV "Epiphanes" ("god made visible"), who set up a pagan altar in the Jerusalem temple in 167 BC (1 Maccabees 1:54). The Ascension of Isaiah, a Christian writing from about John's time, says the devil Beliar will come "in the form of a man, a lawless king, the murderer of his mother," clearly Nero (Ascension of Isaiah 4:2). The Didache warns that "the world-deceiver will appear as a son of God" (16:4).`],
  ['Candidates in history',`Nero; Domitian; Muhammad (medieval Christians); various popes (Reformers); Napoleon; Mussolini; Hitler; Stalin; and many modern politicians. Every confident identification so far has been wrong.`]],
 common:[`Revelation describes "the Antichrist" by name.`],
 uncommon:[`John's own letters define antichrist as a spirit of denial already at work, not only a single future ruler.`],
 claude:{title:'Look for the counterfeit, not the person',body:`John's letters say the antichrist spirit "denies that Jesus came in the flesh" (1 John 4:2–3). Revelation's beast is a counterfeit Lamb: slaughtered-and-healed (13:3), worshiped, given authority by its "father" the dragon (13:2, 4). Reading both together, I think John cares less about naming one man than about training readers to spot any power that imitates Christ's death and resurrection to claim worship. That makes the warning permanent rather than only future.`, refs:'1 John 2:18; 1 John 4:2–3; 13:2–4; 5:6'},
 views:{P:`Nero, or the Roman emperors in general.`,H:`The papacy as an institution (the classic Protestant view).`,F:`A single future world ruler who will make a covenant with Israel and then break it.`,I:`The spirit of counterfeit Christ present in every age, perhaps culminating in a final figure.`},
 links:[['Antichrist','Antichrist'],['Man of sin','Man of sin'],['Little horn','Little horn'],['Antiochus IV Epiphanes','Antiochus IV Epiphanes']],
 sources:['koesterh','bauckham','irenaeus']});

TP({id:'mark', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Mark of the Beast', ch:[13,14,16,19,20],
 lead:`A <i>charagma</i>, a stamp or engraved mark, "on" the right hand or forehead, needed to buy or sell (13:16–17). It appears seven times in the book, always in connection with worship of the beast.`,
 sections:[
  ['What a charagma was',`The word was used for the emperor's image stamped on coins, for official seals on contracts and bills of sale, and for brands on animals and slaves. Every coin in a Christian's purse carried the emperor's image and titles, sometimes "son of the divine." Some historians link the mark to the imperial cult: trade guilds in Asia held feasts honoring the emperor and local gods, and refusing could cost a person their livelihood.`],
  ['Its mirror image',`God's servants are sealed "on their foreheads" (7:3) and carry the Lamb's and Father's name there (14:1; 22:4). The Shema told Israel to bind God's words "on your hand" and "between your eyes" (Deuteronomy 6:8), which observant Jews still do with tefillin. The high priest wore "Holy to the LORD" on his forehead (Exodus 28:36–38). The mark of the beast is the opposite of all three.`],
  {h:'The mark in Revelation', table:[['Verse','Context'],['13:16–17','Required to buy or sell'],['14:9–11','Those who take it drink God’s anger'],['15:2','The victors overcame the beast, its image and its number'],['16:2','The first bowl: sores on those with the mark'],['19:20','The false prophet deceived those who received it'],['20:4','The martyrs had not received it']]},
  ['Modern guesses',`Social Security numbers, credit cards, barcodes, RFID chips, vaccines, and digital IDs have all been proposed. The KJV's "in their right hand" (Greek <i>epi</i>, "on") helped the implant idea. Each new technology has drawn the same fear.`]],
 common:[`The mark is a technology that someone could take by accident.`],
 uncommon:[`Every person in Revelation is marked by someone. The mark is about whom you worship (14:9–11), so no one takes it by accident.`],
 claude:{title:'The mark is a counterfeit priesthood',body:`In Revelation every believer becomes a priest (1:6; 5:10; 20:6), and the priest's mark was on the forehead (Exodus 28:36–38). The final promise is "his name will be on their foreheads" (22:4). Since the beast's mark is also on the forehead, I read it as a rival priesthood: people who serve and represent the beast in daily life. That explains why it is tied to buying and selling, the daily "liturgy" of empire.`, refs:'13:16–17; 22:4; Exodus 28:36–38; 1:6'},
 views:{P:`Participation in emperor worship and the Roman economy in the first century.`,H:`Allegiance to the papacy (some Adventists: Sunday-keeping enforced by law).`,F:`A literal, perhaps technological, mark required in the tribulation.`,I:`Visible loyalty to any power that claims what belongs to God, in thought (forehead) and action (hand).`},
 links:[['Number of the beast','Number of the beast'],['Imperial cult of ancient Rome','Imperial cult of ancient Rome'],['Shema Yisrael','Shema Yisrael'],['Tefillin','Tefillin']],
 sources:['koester','friesen','bauckham']});

TP({id:'666', kind:'Symbols', title:'666, the Number of the Beast', ch:[13],
 lead:`"Whoever has understanding should figure out the number of the beast, because it is a human number. Its number is 666" (13:18). John expects his readers to be able to work it out.`,
 sections:[
  ['Gematria',`In Hebrew and Greek, letters were also numbers, so any name had a number. A graffito in Pompeii reads "I love her whose number is 545." The Sibylline Oracles give Jesus's name the number 888 (<i>Iēsous</i>: 10 + 8 + 200 + 70 + 400 + 200), one more than seven three times over, against the beast's one less. In Hebrew letters, Nero Caesar (נרון קסר) adds to 666; the Latin spelling (נרו קסר) adds to 616, the number in some manuscripts. See the page on Nero.`],
  ['The 616 variant',`Papyrus 115 (about AD 300), the oldest copy of this verse, and Codex Ephraemi read 616. Irenaeus knew both and defended 666 as found in "all the most approved and ancient copies" (<i>Against Heresies</i> 5.30.1).`],
  {h:'Famous decodings', table:[['Proposal','Basis','Proposed by'],['Nero Caesar','Hebrew letters total 666 (or 616)','Many modern scholars'],['<i>Lateinos</i> ("the Latin one")','Greek letters total 666','Irenaeus, as one possibility'],['<i>Teitan</i>','Greek letters total 666','Irenaeus’s own preference'],['<i>Vicarius Filii Dei</i>','Roman numerals in a supposed papal title total 666','Some Protestants and Adventists'],['Muhammad (<i>Maometis</i>)','A Greek spelling totaling 666','Some medieval writers'],['Napoleon, Hitler, and many others','Various spellings and systems','Popular writers']],
   note:'With enough flexibility in spelling and system, almost any name can be made to reach 666, which is why the Nero solution is valued: it uses ordinary Hebrew spelling and explains the 616 variant.'},
  ['Other readings',`Six falls short of seven, so 666 is "always falling short." It is also a triangular number (the sum of 1 to 36), and 36 is itself the sum of 1 to 8, which some link to the beast as "an eighth" king (17:11). Some link it to the 666 talents of gold that came to Solomon each year (1 Kings 10:14).`]],
 common:[`666 is a code for a future person we must decode.`],
 uncommon:[`The first readers likely could decode it, because John says they could.`],
 claude:{title:'666 and the king who broke the king’s law',body:`1 Kings 10 is the chapter with 666 talents. It goes on to list exactly what Deuteronomy 17:16–17 forbade a king of Israel to pile up: gold, horses from Egypt, and (in chapter 11) many wives. It describes Solomon controlling the horse and chariot trade (1 Kings 10:28–29), just as the beast controls buying and selling (13:17). My speculation: "the number of a man" points to the moment a king of God's own people turned into a Pharaoh. The beast is what power becomes when it grasps at wealth, weapons and control.`, refs:'1 Kings 10:14; 1 Kings 10:28–29; Deuteronomy 17:16–17; 13:17–18'},
 views:{P:`Nero Caesar.`,H:`The papacy or its titles.`,F:`The name of a future Antichrist, to be recognized when he appears.`,I:`The number of humanity falling short of God, always pretending to be divine.`},
 links:[['Number of the beast','Number of the beast'],['Gematria','Gematria'],['Papyrus 115','Papyrus 115']],
 sources:['metzger','bauckham','koester']});

TP({id:'first-resurrection', kind:'Ideas', title:'The First Resurrection', ch:[20],
 lead:`"They came to life and ruled with Christ for a thousand years... This is the first resurrection. Blessed and holy is the one who has a share in the first resurrection. The second death has no power over them" (20:4–6).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Three readings', table:[['Reading','The first resurrection is','Held by'],['Bodily, future','A real bodily resurrection of believers at Christ’s return, 1,000 years before the rest','Premillennialists'],['Spiritual, present','New birth: passing from death to life now (John 5:24–25; Ephesians 2:5–6)','Augustine and many amillennialists'],['Heavenly, at death','The martyrs and faithful going to reign with Christ when they die','Many amillennialists']]},
  ['The Greek',`<i>Ezēsan</i>, "they lived" or "they came to life." The same verb is used of Jesus's resurrection in 2:8 ("who died and came to life") and of the beast's recovery in 13:14. Premillennialists argue it must mean bodily resurrection in 20:4 because it plainly does in 20:5 ("the rest of the dead did not come to life"). Amillennialists answer that John pairs a first and second resurrection with a first and second death, and the first death is physical while the second is not.`],
  ['Who is raised?',`John sees "the souls of those who had been beheaded because of their testimony about Jesus" and those who had not worshiped the beast (20:4). Beheading was the Roman punishment for citizens and the method of execution tradition gives for Paul. The thrones and judgment come from Daniel 7:9, 22: "judgment was given in favor of the holy people."`],
  ['A blessing',`"Blessed and holy" (20:6) is the fifth of Revelation's seven blessings. They will be "priests of God and of Christ," fulfilling 1:6 and 5:10.`]],
 views:{P:`The vindication of the martyrs after AD 70, reigning with Christ now.`,H:`A revival of the true church in history.`,F:`The bodily resurrection of believers (or tribulation martyrs) at Christ's return.`,I:`New life in Christ and the reign of believers with Christ, in this life and after death.`},
 links:[['Resurrection of the dead','Resurrection of the dead'],['Millennialism','Millennialism']],
 sources:['ladd','beale','gregg','augustine']});

TP({id:'white-throne', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Great White Throne', ch:[20],
 lead:`The final judgment scene (20:11–15). Earth and sky flee from the face of the one on the throne, the books are opened, and the dead, "great and small," are judged "by what they had done."`,
 sections:[
  ['The scene',`"White" is the color of purity and victory throughout the book. "Earth and sky fled from his presence, and there was no place for them" (20:11) clears the stage: nothing stands between the judge and the judged. The sea, Death and Hades all give up their dead (20:13): no one is lost, and no one escapes.`],
  ['Books and the book',`"Books" record deeds; "the book of life" records names (20:12, 15). The picture comes from Daniel 7:10 ("the court was seated, and the books were opened"). Malachi speaks of "a scroll of remembrance" (Malachi 3:16), and a psalm asks God to record tears in his book (Psalm 56:8). Revelation holds both: deeds matter, and belonging to the Lamb matters most (13:8; 21:27).`],
  ['Judged by works?',`Many readers ask how judgment "by what they had done" fits salvation by grace. Paul says the same two things: God "will repay each person according to what they have done" (Romans 2:6), and "it is by grace you have been saved... not by works" (Ephesians 2:8–9). Jesus's parable of the sheep and goats judges by deeds of mercy (Matthew 25:31–46). Most traditions say deeds are the evidence of what a person has become, and the book of life is the ground of rescue.`],
  ['Who sits on the throne?',`John says "him who was seated on it" (20:11), normally God the Father. Elsewhere the New Testament says the Father "has entrusted all judgment to the Son" (John 5:22; 2 Corinthians 5:10). By 22:1 the throne is "the throne of God and of the Lamb."`],
  ['In art',`The last judgment has been painted and carved more than almost any other scene: over the doors of medieval churches (Autun, Conques), by Giotto in Padua (about 1305), and by Michelangelo on the altar wall of the Sistine Chapel (1536–41).`]],
 views:{P:`Partial preterists: a future final judgment. Full preterists: fulfilled in AD 70.`,H:`The final judgment at the end of history.`,F:`A judgment of the unbelieving dead after the millennium; believers were judged earlier.`,I:`The final judgment of all people at the end.`},
 links:[['Last Judgment','Last Judgment'],['The Last Judgment (Michelangelo)','The Last Judgment (Michelangelo)']],
 sources:['beale','keener']});

TP({id:'lake-of-fire', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Lake of Fire', ch:[14,19,20,21],
 lead:`The final destination of the beast, the false prophet, the devil, Death and Hades, and those not found in the book of life (19:20; 20:10, 14–15; 21:8). <button class="linkish" data-go="tp-second-death">The second death</button> discusses the views in more depth.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The lake in Revelation', table:[['Verse','Who is thrown in'],['19:20','The beast and the false prophet, "alive"'],['20:10','The devil; "tormented day and night forever and ever"'],['20:14','Death and Hades; "this is the second death"'],['20:15','Anyone whose name was not found in the book of life'],['21:8','The cowardly, the unfaithful, murderers, liars and others']]},
  ['Views',`<b>Eternal conscious punishment</b>, the traditional view (14:11; 20:10). <b>Conditional immortality</b> or annihilation: the "second death" means final destruction (John Stott, Edward Fudge). <b>Christian universalism</b>: final restoration even of the lost, often citing the open gates and the kings bringing in their glory (21:24–25), a minority view from Origen to modern writers.`],
  ['Background',`Sodom's "burning sulfur" (Genesis 19:24); Isaiah's "the fire that will not be quenched" (Isaiah 66:24); Daniel's beast "thrown into the blazing fire" (Daniel 7:11). Gehenna, the valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem where children had been sacrificed to Molech (2 Kings 23:10; Jeremiah 7:31–32), became Jesus's word for the place of final judgment (Matthew 5:22; Mark 9:43–48). 1 Enoch speaks of an abyss "full of fire" for the fallen angels (1 Enoch 90:24–26).`],
  {myth:{culture:'Egypt', title:'The lake of fire in the underworld', body:`The Egyptian <i>Book of the Dead</i> (spell 126) and tomb paintings show a lake of fire in the underworld, guarded by four baboons, which burns the wicked and refreshes the righteous. Greek myth had Pyriphlegethon, a river of fire in Hades. There is no evidence John borrowed from Egypt; fire as the picture of final judgment was common across the ancient world and already present in the Old Testament.`, link:['Lake of fire','Lake of fire'], link2:'a parallel, no known link'}}],
 common:[`Revelation gives a detailed map of hell.`],
 uncommon:[`Death and Hades themselves are thrown into the lake (20:14). The image is at least partly about the end of death itself.`],
 links:[['Lake of fire','Lake of fire'],['Second death','Second death'],['Annihilationism','Annihilationism'],['Gehenna','Gehenna']],
 sources:['koester','beale']});

TP({id:'book-of-life', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Book of Life', ch:[3,13,17,20,21,22],
 lead:`A register of God's people, like a city's list of citizens. "I will never erase that person's name from the book of life" (3:5). Only those "whose names are written in the Lamb's book of life" enter the city (21:27).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The book in Revelation', table:[['Verse','What it says'],['3:5','The one who wins will never be erased from it'],['13:8','Those not written in it worship the beast; "the Lamb who was slaughtered since the foundation of the world"'],['17:8','Those not written in it "since the foundation of the world" are amazed at the beast'],['20:12','"Another book was opened, which is the book of life"'],['20:15','Anyone not found written in it is thrown into the lake'],['21:27','Only those written in it enter the city'],['22:19','(KJV "book of life"; Greek "tree of life")']]},
  ['Background',`Moses prayed, "Blot me out of the book you have written" (Exodus 32:32–33). A psalm asks that enemies "be blotted out of the book of life" (Psalm 69:28). Isaiah speaks of those "recorded among the living in Jerusalem" (Isaiah 4:3), Daniel of everyone "whose name is found written in the book" (Daniel 12:1), and Jesus told his disciples to rejoice "that your names are written in heaven" (Luke 10:20; also Philippians 4:3; Hebrews 12:23).`],
  ['Citizen rolls',`Greek cities kept registers of citizens, and a citizen condemned for a serious crime could have his name erased. Sardis's readers, in a city of proud old families, would have heard "I will never erase your name" (3:5) as a promise of citizenship that cannot be revoked.`],
  ['The Jewish New Year',`Jewish tradition says that on Rosh Hashanah three books are opened: one for the righteous, one for the wicked, and one for those in between (Talmud, Rosh Hashanah 16b). The traditional greeting for the season is "May you be inscribed in the book of life." Revelation's "books were opened" belongs to the same picture.`],
  ['"Since the world was made"',`13:8 and 17:8 say names were written "since the foundation of the world," a key text in debates about predestination. The Greek order of 13:8 can also mean "the Lamb slaughtered since the foundation of the world" (KJV). Calvinists stress God's choice before creation; Arminians stress that names can be "blotted out" (3:5; Exodus 32:33) and that the promise is to "the one who wins."`],
  ['A textual slip',`KJV 22:19 says "book of life" where nearly every Greek manuscript says "tree of life," because of Erasmus's back-translation from Latin in 1516.`]],
 links:[['Book of Life','Book of Life'],['Rosh Hashanah','Rosh Hashanah']],
 sources:['metzger','koester','hemer']});

TP({id:'new-creation', kind:'Ideas', title:'New Heaven and New Earth', ch:[21,22],
 lead:`"Then I saw a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away, and the sea was no more" (21:1), quoting Isaiah 65:17 and 66:22. The Bible ends where it began: with heaven, earth, a garden, a river and a tree.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Seven things that are gone', table:[['No more...','Verse','What it undoes'],['Sea','21:1','Chaos, the beast’s home, separation'],['Death','21:4','The "last enemy" (1 Corinthians 15:26)'],['Mourning','21:4','Grief for the dead'],['Crying','21:4','"God will wipe every tear from their eyes" (Isaiah 25:8)'],['Pain','21:4','Suffering of every kind'],['Curse','22:3','The curse of Genesis 3:17–19'],['Night','21:25; 22:5','Darkness and danger']]},
  ['Renewed or replaced?',`<i>Kainos</i>, "new," often means new in quality rather than brand new. Some read total replacement: "the heavens will disappear with a roar; the elements will be destroyed by fire" (2 Peter 3:10–12). But the oldest manuscripts of 2 Peter 3:10 end with "the earth and everything done in it will be laid bare" (found, exposed), not "burned up." Many read renewal: creation "will be liberated from its bondage to decay" (Romans 8:19–23). "I am making everything new" (21:5) says <i>everything</i> new, not new things.`],
  ['Genesis to Revelation', `The garden becomes a city, but the garden is still inside it: a river (Genesis 2:10; Revelation 22:1), the tree of life (Genesis 2:9; Revelation 22:2), gold and precious stones (Genesis 2:12; Revelation 21:18–21), God walking with people (Genesis 3:8; Revelation 21:3), and the curse removed (Genesis 3:17; Revelation 22:3).`],
  ['Heaven comes down',`In the end, people do not go up to heaven: the city comes down, and "God's home is now with people" (21:3). The story ends on a renewed earth. Writers such as N. T. Wright (<i>Surprised by Hope</i>, 2008) have made this point popular: the Christian hope is not escape from the world but its renewal.`]],
 common:[`The Christian hope is to leave the earth and live in heaven.`],
 uncommon:[`Revelation's final scene is God moving in with humanity on a renewed earth.`],
 views:{P:`The new covenant order that began after the old one ended in AD 70, to be completed at the end.`,H:`The eternal state after the end of history.`,F:`A literal new universe after the millennium.`,I:`The final renewal of all things, already tasted in the church.`},
 links:[['New Heaven and New Earth','New Heaven and New Earth'],['World to come','World to come']],
 sources:['bauckham','koester']});

TP({id:'worship', kind:'Ideas', title:'Worship and the Songs of Revelation', ch:[4,5,7,11,14,15,19],
 lead:`Revelation has more songs than any other New Testament book. Many church hymns ("Holy, Holy, Holy," "Crown Him with Many Crowns," Handel's "Hallelujah" chorus, "Worthy Is the Lamb") come from it.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The songs', table:[['Verses','Singers','Theme'],['4:8','Four living creatures','"Holy, holy, holy"'],['4:11','24 elders','God is worthy as Creator'],['5:9–10','Creatures and elders','The new song: the Lamb is worthy because he was slaughtered'],['5:12–13','Angels; then every creature','"Worthy is the Lamb"'],['7:10–12','The great crowd; angels','"Salvation belongs to our God"'],['11:15–18','Loud voices; the elders','"The kingdom of the world has become the kingdom of our Lord"'],['12:10–12','A loud voice in heaven','The accuser is thrown down'],['15:3–4','The victors by the sea','The song of Moses and of the Lamb'],['16:5–7','The angel of the waters; the altar','"True and just are your judgments"'],['19:1–8','The crowd, elders, creatures','The four hallelujahs; the wedding of the Lamb']]},
  ['Songs explain the story',`Each song comes right before or after a judgment and tells the reader what it means. The book is like a Greek play in which the chorus explains the action.`],
  ['Worship as resistance',`In Asia, people were asked to honor the emperor as "lord and god." Pergamum even had a choir of "hymn singers of the god Augustus" who sang at imperial festivals. Revelation's songs give the emperors' titles only to God and the Lamb: "You are worthy, our Lord and God" (4:11). Choosing whom to worship is the book's central decision (13:4, 8; 14:7, 9).`],
  ['Incense and prayer',`The gold bowls of incense "are the prayers of God's people" (5:8). In 8:3–5 those prayers go up and fire comes down: prayer moves history.`]],
 claude:{title:'Silence is the answer to "How long?"',body:`The martyrs ask "How long?" (6:10). After the sixth seal, heaven falls silent for half an hour (8:1), then incense with "the prayers of all God's people" rises (8:3–4). In the temple, incense was offered while the people prayed outside (Luke 1:10), and later Jewish tradition says the angels' singing stopped so Israel's prayers could be heard. My reading: the silence is heaven pausing everything to listen to the martyrs, and the trumpets are the answer. The incense link is noted by Bauckham; the half-hour as a deliberate "listening" pause is my emphasis.`, refs:'6:10; 8:1–5; Luke 1:10; Psalm 141:2'},
 links:[['Holy, Holy, Holy (hymn)','Holy, Holy, Holy! Lord God Almighty'],['Hallelujah Chorus','Hallelujah (Handel)'],['Crown Him with Many Crowns','Crown Him with Many Crowns']],
 sources:['bauckham','climax','friesen']});

TP({id:'martyrs', kind:'Ideas', title:'Martyrs and Witnesses', ch:[2,6,11,12,17,20],
 lead:`The Greek <i>martys</i> means "witness," someone who testifies in court. By the time Revelation was written, it was starting to mean someone who witnessed to the point of death. Revelation is one of the places where that change can be seen happening.`,
 sections:[
  ['In Revelation',`Jesus is "the faithful witness" (1:5; 3:14). Antipas is "my faithful witness, who was killed" (2:13). Martyrs are under the altar (6:9), the two witnesses are killed (11:7), Babylon is "drunk with the blood of the witnesses to Jesus" (17:6), and the saints win "by the word of their testimony" and by not loving their lives (12:11). Those beheaded "because of their testimony" reign with Christ (20:4).`],
  ['Under the altar',`The souls of the martyrs are "under the altar" (6:9), where the blood of sacrifices was poured (Leviticus 4:7). Their deaths are pictured as offerings to God. They are told to "rest a little longer, until the number of their fellow servants... was complete" (6:11), a hard word that expects more suffering before the end.`],
  {h:'Martyrs connected to Revelation’s churches', table:[['Name','Place','Date','Notes'],['Antipas','Pergamum','Before about 95','Named in 2:13; tradition says roasted in a bronze bull'],['Ignatius of Antioch','Rome','About 110','Wrote on the way to letters to Ephesus, Smyrna and Philadelphia'],['Polycarp','Smyrna','About 155','Knew John; burned at age 86'],['Carpus, Papylus and Agathonice','Pergamum','About 161–169 or 250','Their trial record survives'],['Perpetua and Felicity','Carthage, North Africa','203','A young mother’s own prison diary survives']]},
  ['"The blood of Christians is seed"',`Tertullian, about AD 197, wrote to Roman officials: "The more you mow us down, the more we grow; the blood of Christians is seed" (<i>Apology</i> 50). That is very close to Revelation's logic: witnesses die and are vindicated, and their death converts where plagues could not (11:11–13).`],
  ['How many?',`Some scholars think Revelation expects widespread martyrdom; others note it names only one martyr (Antipas) and is warning about what faithfulness may cost. Roman persecution was mostly local and occasional until the empire-wide campaigns of Decius (250) and Diocletian (303–311).`]],
 views:{P:`The martyrs of Nero's persecution and the first century.`,H:`Martyrs throughout church history, including Protestants killed by the Inquisition.`,F:`Especially the believers martyred during the tribulation.`,I:`Faithful witnesses of every age, whose deaths are victories.`},
 links:[['Christian martyrs','Christian martyrs'],['Antipas of Pergamum','Antipas of Pergamum'],['Perpetua and Felicity','Perpetua and Felicity']],
 sources:['bauckham','blount']});

TP({id:'repentance', kind:'Ideas', title:'Repentance', ch:[2,3,9,16],
 lead:`<i>Metanoeō</i>, "turn around, change your mind." Revelation uses it twelve times: eight times to churches (chapters 2–3), four times of the world that does not repent (9:20–21; 16:9, 11).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Every call to repent', table:[['Verse','Who','About what'],['2:5 (twice)','Ephesus','Lost first love: "repent and do the works you did at first"'],['2:16','Pergamum','Tolerating Balaam’s teaching'],['2:21 (twice)','Jezebel','Sexual sin; she "refuses to repent"'],['2:22','Her followers','"Unless they repent of her works"'],['3:3','Sardis','Remember, obey, repent'],['3:19','Laodicea','"Those I love I rebuke and discipline. So be earnest and repent"'],['9:20, 21','The world','Did not repent of idols, murders, magic, sexual sin, theft'],['16:9, 11','The world','Cursed God and did not repent']],
   note:'More calls to repent go to churches than to the world. The book’s first audience is the church.'},
  ['The idea',`In the Old Testament the word is <i>shuv</i>, "turn back," the word of every prophet: "Turn back, turn back from your evil ways! Why will you die, people of Israel?" (Ezekiel 33:11). The Greek word adds "change of mind." In Revelation repentance always involves a concrete change: "do the works you did at first" (2:5).`],
  ['The point of the plagues',`The trumpets strike only a third, and the narrator twice notes that people "still did not turn away" (9:20–21). The judgments are meant to bring people back. Even Jezebel was given "time to repent" (2:21).`],
  ['Does it work?',`Yes, once: after the two witnesses rise, the survivors "were terrified and gave glory to the God of heaven" (11:13), the very response the first angel calls for in 14:7. Richard Bauckham argues this is the book's turning point: witness converts where plagues could not. In the earthquake only a tenth of the city falls and 7,000 die, a reversal of Elijah's story where 7,000 were spared and the rest perished (1 Kings 19:18).`]],
 links:[['Repentance','Repentance'],['Metanoia','Metanoia (theology)']],
 sources:['bauckham','climax']});

TP({id:'soon', kind:'Ideas', title:'"Soon" and "Near"', ch:[1,2,3,22],
 lead:`The book begins and ends with urgency: "what must happen soon" (1:1; 22:6), "the time is near" (1:3; 22:10), "I am coming soon" (22:7, 12, 20). Two thousand years later, every reader has to decide what these words meant.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The time words', table:[['Greek','Meaning','Verses'],['<i>en tachei</i>','"soon," "quickly," "without delay"','1:1; 22:6'],['<i>engys</i>','"near"','1:3; 22:10'],['<i>tachy</i>','"soon," "quickly"','2:16; 3:11; 22:7, 12, 20'],['<i>mellei</i>','"is about to"','1:19; 3:10; 17:8']]},
  ['The problem',`C. S. Lewis called Jesus's saying "this generation will certainly not pass away until all these things have happened" (Mark 13:30) "the most embarrassing verse in the Bible" (<i>The World's Last Night</i>, 1960), and he took it seriously rather than explaining it away. Revelation's "soon" raises the same question.`],
  ['Answers',`<b>Preterist:</b> soon meant soon; much happened in the first century. <b>Futurist:</b> "quickly once it begins," or soon by God's measure: "With the Lord a day is like a thousand years... The Lord is not slow in keeping his promise... he is patient with you" (2 Peter 3:8–9). <b>Idealist:</b> every generation stands near the end, because the decisive victory (the cross) is already won. Some point out that Daniel was told to seal his book because the end was far (Daniel 12:4, 9), while John is told not to seal his (22:10).`],
  ['How it shapes life',`Whatever "soon" means for dates, it means urgency for choices: "Blessed is the one who keeps the words of the prophecy" (22:7). The book's response to "I am coming soon" is not calculation but prayer: "Amen. Come, Lord Jesus" (22:20).`]],
 links:[['Imminence','Imminence'],['C. S. Lewis','C. S. Lewis']],
 sources:['gregg','koester']});

TP({id:'exodus-pattern', kind:'Ideas', title:'The New Exodus', ch:[1,8,9,11,12,15,16],
 lead:`Revelation retells the Exodus. God's people are freed from a new Pharaoh by the blood of a Lamb, carried through the wilderness on eagles' wings, and sing on the far shore of a sea.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The parallels', table:[['Exodus','Revelation'],['The Passover lamb; blood on the doors (12)','The slaughtered Lamb; washed in his blood (5:6, 9; 7:14)'],['Pharaoh, the oppressor king','The beast'],['A kingdom of priests (19:6)','"A kingdom and priests" (1:6; 5:10)'],['Plagues: blood, frogs, sores, hail, locusts, darkness (7–10)','Trumpets and bowls: blood, frogs, sores, hail, locusts, darkness (8–9; 16)'],['"Let my people go"','"Come out of her, my people" (18:4)'],['Eagles’ wings; fed in the wilderness (19:4; 16)','The woman given eagle’s wings, fed in the wilderness (12:6, 14)'],['The song of Moses by the sea (15)','The song of Moses and the Lamb by the sea of glass (15:2–3)'],['Thunder, lightning, trumpet at Sinai (19:16)','Lightning, thunder before the throne (4:5)'],['The tabernacle filled with glory (40:34–35)','The temple filled with smoke and glory (15:8)'],['Manna in the wilderness (16)','Hidden manna (2:17)'],['The promised land','The new Jerusalem']]},
  ['What it means',`The point is not that history repeats but that God acts the same way: he hears the cry of the oppressed (Exodus 3:7; Revelation 6:10) and brings them out. For first-century Christians under Rome, the Exodus told them who they were: not subjects of the empire but a people on the way out of slavery.`],
  ['A hardened heart',`Pharaoh's heart was hardened through the plagues. Revelation notes four times that people "did not repent" (9:20–21; 16:9, 11). In both stories the plagues reveal hearts as much as they punish.`]],
 links:[['The Exodus','The Exodus'],['Plagues of Egypt','Plagues of Egypt']],
 sources:['bauckham','beale']});

TP({id:'imperial-cult', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Imperial Cult', ch:[2,3,13,17,18],
 lead:`The worship of Roman emperors, living or dead, alongside the goddess Roma. It was especially strong in the province of Asia, where all seven churches were, and it is the most likely background for the beast, its image and its mark.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Emperor worship in the seven cities', table:[['City','Imperial temple or honor'],['Pergamum','First temple in Asia to Rome and Augustus (29 BC); a choir of "hymn singers of the god Augustus"'],['Smyrna','Temple to Tiberius, Livia and the Senate (won in AD 26)'],['Ephesus','Temple of the Flavian emperors (about AD 89–90), with a colossal statue'],['Sardis','Honors to Tiberius after the earthquake; a later imperial temple'],['Philadelphia','Renamed Neocaesarea and Flavia after emperors'],['Laodicea','Imperial festivals and games'],['Thyatira','Priests of the emperors attested in inscriptions']]},
  ['"Good news" about a god',`In 9 BC the cities of Asia changed their calendar so the year began on Augustus's birthday. An inscription from Priene, near Ephesus, says the birthday of "the god" was "the beginning of the good news (<i>euangelia</i>) for the world." Christians proclaimed a different "good news" about a different Son of God.`],
  ['Pressure on Christians',`Not worshiping meant skipping city festivals and trade-guild meals, which looked disloyal and could cost work. Pliny's letter to Trajan (about AD 112) describes Christians in nearby Bithynia brought to trial; those who refused to offer incense and wine to the emperor's image and curse Christ were executed (<i>Letters</i> 10.96). Pliny notes that "those who are really Christians cannot be forced" to do these things.`],
  ['In Revelation',`The land beast enforces worship of the sea beast and its image (13:12–15). The songs give "worthy," "Lord and God," and "Savior" to God alone, titles used of emperors. The question "Who is like the beast?" (13:4) parodies Israel's song, "Who is like you, LORD, among the gods?" (Exodus 15:11).`]],
 views:{P:`The direct background of chapters 13 and 17.`,H:`The model for later persecuting powers, especially the papacy.`,F:`A preview of a future world religion centered on the Antichrist.`,I:`The permanent temptation to treat political power as divine.`},
 links:[['Imperial cult of ancient Rome','Imperial cult of ancient Rome'],['Pliny the Younger on Christians','Pliny the Younger'],['Temple of the Sebastoi','Temple of the Sebastoi'],['Priene calendar inscription','Priene calendar inscription']],
 sources:['friesen','koester','hemer']});

TP({id:'overcomer', kind:'Ideas', title:'"The One Who Wins the Victory"', ch:[2,3,5,12,15,17,21],
 lead:`<i>Ho nikōn</i>, "the one who conquers," ends every letter (2:7, 11, 17, 26; 3:5, 12, 21). The same verb is used of the Lamb (5:5) and of the beast (11:7; 13:7). Revelation is about two kinds of winning.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The seven promises and their fulfillment', table:[['Church','Promise','Fulfilled'],['Ephesus (2:7)','Eat from the tree of life','The tree in the city (22:2)'],['Smyrna (2:11)','Not harmed by the second death','"The second death has no power over them" (20:6)'],['Pergamum (2:17)','Hidden manna, white stone, new name','"His name will be on their foreheads" (22:4)'],['Thyatira (2:26–28)','Authority over nations; the morning star','Reign forever (22:5); Jesus the morning star (22:16)'],['Sardis (3:5)','White clothes; name kept in the book of life','Only those in the Lamb’s book enter (21:27)'],['Philadelphia (3:12)','A pillar in God’s temple; the city’s name','The new Jerusalem comes down (21:2, 10)'],['Laodicea (3:21)','Sit with Jesus on his throne','"They will reign forever and ever" (22:5)']],
   note:'The final summary: "The one who wins the victory will inherit all this" (21:7).'},
  ['How you win',`Not by force. "They defeated him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony. They did not love their lives so much that they were afraid to die" (12:11). The beast "conquers" the saints by killing them (13:7), and in the same act they conquer the beast (15:2). The Lamb "has won the victory" (5:5) by being slaughtered.`],
  ['Victory in Roman eyes',`Nike, the winged goddess of victory, was everywhere in the Roman world: on coins, monuments and military standards. Emperors celebrated victories with parades and arches. Athletes who won at the games received wreaths (<i>stephanoi</i>), the word Revelation uses for "crowns" (2:10; 3:11). John takes the empire's favorite word and redefines it.`]],
 links:[['Nike (mythology)','Nike (mythology)'],['Victoria (mythology)','Victoria (mythology)']],
 sources:['bauckham','gorman']});

TP({id:'dating', kind:'Ideas', title:'When Was Revelation Written?', ch:[1,11,13,17],
 lead:`Two main dates: about AD 95 under Domitian, or the late 60s under Nero, before Jerusalem fell. The answer matters a great deal for the preterist view, which reads much of the book as a prophecy of AD 70.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The evidence', table:[['Evidence','Points to'],['Irenaeus: the vision was seen "toward the end of Domitian’s reign"','About AD 95'],['Clement of Alexandria, Eusebius: John returned from exile after "the tyrant" died','About AD 96'],['Laodicea is rich and needs nothing, after its AD 60 earthquake','Later, when it had rebuilt'],['Smyrna’s church seems well established; Polycarp later implies it did not exist in Paul’s day','Later'],['The imperial cult in Asia is strong; Ephesus’s Flavian temple built about 89–90','80s–90s'],['"Babylon" as a name for Rome appears in Jewish writings only after AD 70','After 70'],['John measures a temple that seems to be standing (11:1–2)','Before 70'],['"Five kings have fallen, one is" (17:10), if counted from Augustus or Julius','Nero, Galba or Vespasian (60s–70s)'],['666 fits Nero Caesar','Possibly 60s, or later using Nero as a pattern'],['Some read Irenaeus’s "it was seen" as "he (John) was seen"','Ambiguous']]},
  ['The late date (about AD 95)',`Irenaeus (about AD 180), who knew Polycarp, who knew John, says the vision was seen "toward the end of Domitian's reign" (<i>Against Heresies</i> 5.30.3). The churches seem well established, and the imperial cult in Asia matches the 80s–90s. Most scholars hold this date.`],
  ['The early date (AD 64–70)',`John measures a temple that seems still standing (11:1–2). The seven kings could make the sixth king Nero or Galba. Preterists like Kenneth Gentry argue for this date, and it was common among scholars in the 1800s.`]],
 links:[['Book of Revelation: date','Book of Revelation'],['Domitian','Domitian'],['Nero','Nero']],
 sources:['gentry','koester','irenaeus','aune']});

TP({id:'textual-history', kind:'Words', title:'How the Text Came Down to Us', ch:[13,22],
 lead:`Revelation has fewer Greek manuscripts than any other New Testament book, about 310, and it was not read in Eastern church services. Its text has some famous puzzles.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Important manuscripts', table:[['Manuscript','Date','Notes'],['Papyrus 98','Perhaps 100s','A few verses of chapter 1; possibly the oldest'],['Papyrus 47','200s','Large parts of 9:10–17:2'],['Papyrus 115','About 300','Fragments of 2–15; reads 616 in 13:18'],['Codex Sinaiticus','300s','The oldest complete Revelation'],['Codex Alexandrinus','400s','Usually considered the best witness for Revelation'],['Codex Ephraemi','400s','A palimpsest: the Bible was scraped off and overwritten, but can still be read'],['Andreas of Caesarea','500s','A Greek commentary whose text shaped most later copies'],['Minuscule 2814','1100s','Erasmus’s only manuscript, missing the last page']]},
  ['Erasmus and the last page',`When Erasmus printed the first Greek New Testament in 1516, he had one manuscript of Revelation (minuscule 2814), and it was missing the last page, 22:16–21. He translated those verses back into Greek from the Latin Vulgate. Several of his readings, such as "book of life" in 22:19, passed into the Textus Receptus and the KJV.`],
  ['Key variants',`666 or 616 (13:18). "Wash their robes" or "do his commandments" (22:14). "The kingdom" or "the kingdoms" (11:15). "I stood" or "he stood" on the sand (12:18 or 13:1). "Them" or "us" (5:10). "Your wife Jezebel" or "the woman Jezebel" (2:20).`],
  ['Which text do Bibles use?',`The KJV and NKJV follow the Textus Receptus. Most modern Bibles (ESV, NIV, NASB, CSB, NLT) follow the critical text (Nestle-Aland, UBS). The SBLGNT shown on this site is a critical text. The World English Bible follows the Majority Text, the reading of most manuscripts.`]],
 links:[['Textus Receptus','Textus Receptus'],['Novum Instrumentum omne','Novum Instrumentum omne'],['Minuscule 2814','Minuscule 2814'],['Codex Alexandrinus','Codex Alexandrinus']],
 sources:['metzger']});
